isrupt critical functions and cause neurological deficits, swelling, and pressure-related symptoms.\n\nIn addition, the **BRAIN** can be evaluated for secondary (metastatic) disease. It is a common distant metastatic site for **breast cancer** and a potential metastatic site for **esophageal cancer**, **thyroid cancer**, and (less commonly than lung/lymph node/bone involvement) **kidney cancer**. Metastatic thyroid cancer in the **BRAIN** may be treated with radiation therapy for local control. MRI of the **BRAIN** may be recommended in certain settings (including suspected metastases and some aggressive cancers such as small cell neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix).\n\nClinically, the **BRAIN** is frequently assessed with imaging: CT is used to evaluate suspected CNS bleeding, and MRI is used to detect tumors and assess swelling, including swelling of the brain’s outer layers in conditions such as leukemic meningitis. Leukemia (including AML) can rarely involve the lining of the **BRAIN** and the fluid surrounding it, sometimes treated with intrathecal chemotherapy. On PET imaging, the **BRAIN** normally appears bright, so increased uptake is not automatically evidence of cancer and must be interpreted cautiously.", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null, "reasoning_content": "**Summarizing brain functions**\n\nI need to provide a summary for the entity BRAIN, covering aspects like its role as a CNS organ, various functions, and related conditions. I should touch on topics like imaging techniques, cancers including metastasis, primary tumors, lymphoma, gliomas, leukemic meningitis, and AML. I'll also mention the blood-brain barrier, the optic nerve connection, and the significance of PET brightness and electrolytes. Keeping it under 500 words and in the third person should help crafting cohesive paragraphs."}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 673, "prompt_tokens": 991, "total_